    category: "science",
    description: "Bioluminescence is the ability of a living thing to produce its own light through a chemical reaction inside its body. It happens when a molecule called luciferin reacts with oxygen, often with help from an enzyme called luciferase, releasing energy as a soft glow rather than heat. This light shows up across very different creatures: fireflies blinking in a summer field, deep-sea anglerfish luring prey in total darkness, and plankton that make ocean waves sparkle blue at night. Each species uses the glow differently. Some use it to attract mates, others to confuse predators, and some to lure unsuspecting prey close enough to catch. In the deep ocean, where sunlight never reaches, bioluminescence is one of the only sources of light at all, and a huge number of deep-sea animals have evolved some version of it. Scientists have also borrowed this glowing trick for practical use, inserting the genes responsible into other cells to light up and track biological processes in labs. What began as a strange glow in the dark has become a genuine tool for modern medical research."
