    description: "Etching is a printmaking technique in which an artist uses acid to carve an image into a metal plate, typically copper or zinc, allowing for the creation of multiple detailed prints from a single original design. The process begins with coating a metal plate in a thin, acid-resistant layer called a ground, into which the artist scratches their design using a sharp needle, exposing the bare metal beneath wherever lines are drawn. When submerged in an acid bath, the exposed metal lines are gradually eaten away, or bitten, into the plate, creating fine grooves that will eventually hold ink, while the protected areas remain smooth and untouched by the acid. Once the design is complete, the plate is inked, with ink settling into the etched grooves, then wiped clean across the surface and pressed firmly against paper using a printing press, transferring the inked design onto the paper in a process that can be repeated to produce numerous nearly identical prints. Etching became especially popular during the seventeenth century, with artists like Rembrandt van Rijn producing extraordinarily detailed and expressive etched works that demonstrated the technique's capacity for fine linework and rich tonal variation. The technique remains widely practiced today by printmakers valuing its distinctive linear quality and capacity for fine, controlled detail."
